<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/foundational/design-is-deciding">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Design, and it assumes nothing — no drawing ability, no
    software, no design vocabulary. You have been a user of designed things your whole
    life: doors, kettles, apps, bus maps. This lesson turns that lifetime of experience
    into a discipline by teaching you to see the decisions hiding inside every product.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what design is — deciding on the user's behalf — and name the three decision families that dominate interfaces: hierarchy, affordance and feedback;</li>
    <li>use Fitts's law to explain why bigger, closer targets are faster to hit, and where its returns flatten;</li>
    <li>analyse user needs as a job under conditions — personas, jobs-to-be-done and context of use — rather than as a demographic sketch;</li>
    <li>apply anthropometrics correctly: percentiles instead of the average, clearance versus reach, adjustability, and inclusive design;</li>
    <li>explain affordance, signifier, mapping and constraints, and why an aesthetic choice is a cognitive choice;</li>
    <li>order the six Rs of sustainability from most to least effective, and connect design decisions to markets, behaviour and ethics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through everything: for every design you meet, ask <em>who decided
    what</em> — what you see first, what happens when you tap, what happens when you are
    wrong. Nothing about a product is neutral; every detail was a decision, and good design
    is making the decisions well.
  </p>

  <h2>The door that lies</h2>
  <p>
    You have pushed a door that said PULL. Everybody has. The door looked like it should be
    pushed — a flat plate with no handle, at hand height — and the printed word was a
    patch on a design failure. Don Norman, the psychologist who named the phenomenon,
    called these <em>Norman doors</em>, and they teach the first lesson of the subject: an
    object tells you how to use it, and when the object's shape contradicts its label, the
    label loses. The failure was not the users'. The failure was a decision — made by a
    designer — about what the door would look like.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the discipline in miniature. Design is not decoration — it is <strong>deciding
    on the user's behalf</strong>: what to see first, what happens when you tap, what
    happens when you are wrong. The craft is making hundreds of small decisions so
    coherently that the interface — or the chair, the kettle, the bus map — feels obvious
    before you think. Three decisions dominate: <strong>hierarchy</strong> (what matters
    most), <strong>affordance</strong> (what looks usable) and <strong>feedback</strong>
    (confirming every action instantly). But those interface-level decisions rest on a
    deeper layer the syllabus names explicitly: every design is an answer to a chain of
    influences — who the user is, where the product will be used, the sizes and abilities
    of real bodies, what looks right in this culture, what the design's life does to the
    planet, and what its existence does to the market and society around it. This lesson
    walks that whole chain, from thumb-reach physics to global waste.
  </p>
  <p>
    We start with the one piece of hard mathematics in the subject, because it shows that
    even "obvious" choices have reasons you can measure.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Fitts's law (1954) — bigger, closer targets are faster to hit; drag the width and the maths agrees" widget="plotter" config={fittsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve plots how long it takes to hit a target 300
    pixels away, as you drag its width. Fitts's law says movement time grows with the
    logarithm of distance-over-size — and the logarithm has a shape worth internalising:
    doubling a button's width matters enormously when it is tiny and almost not at all when
    it is already large. The first 40 pixels are worth more than the next 160. It is why
    phone buttons live at the bottom of the screen (closest to the thumb's arc), why the
    close button on a dialog is smaller than the primary action (the designer is steering
    your choice), and why tiny tap targets are failures with a formula behind them. Design
    is full of returns like this, and knowing where the curve flattens is knowing where to
    stop spending.
  </p>

  <h2>User needs and the context of use</h2>
  <p>
    Everything starts with a disciplined answer to: <em>who needs this, to do what, in
    what situation?</em> "User needs" is not a demographic sketch — it is a job under
    conditions. The same map app is a different product for a pedestrian in Tokyo rain, a
    courier on a bike with gloves on, and a driver at night; each context changes the
    priorities (glanceability, one-handed reach, glare, noise). The professional tools
    are <strong>personas</strong> (concrete archetypes built from research, so the team
    argues about "Amira, 34, commutes on a crowded train" rather than "the user"),
    <strong>jobs-to-be-done</strong> framing (people "hire" a product to do a job — the
    drill bought for the hole), and <strong>context-of-use analysis</strong>: environment
    (lighting, noise, weather, stress), frequency of use (an interface used hourly must be
    fast and muscle-memorised; one used yearly must be self-explanatory — different
    designs entirely), and consequences of error (a music app's undo versus a medical
    pump's dosing error are held to different standards of certainty). A design decision
    made without its context is the classic failure mode: the beautiful interface that
    assumes silence, two free hands and perfect connectivity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> design is deciding, the decisions have measurable physics
    (Fitts), and every decision begins with a job under conditions. Next, the conditions
    get physical — because users come in bodies, and bodies come in ranges.
  </p>

  <h2>Anthropometrics and ergonomics: designing for real bodies</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Anthropometrics</strong> is the measurement of human bodies, and its central
    lesson is the distribution: people vary enormously in height, reach, grip strength and
    visual acuity, so the designer must ask <em>which percentile</em> each dimension is
    being set for. The standard rule — design for the <strong>5th to 95th
    percentile</strong>, never the average — follows from a fact worth internalising: the
    "average person" exists on almost no two dimensions at once, let alone five. When
    Gilbert Daniels measured over 4,000 pilots in 1950 against five body dimensions, he
    found that a cockpit built for the average on all five would fit approximately
    nobody — a finding that grounded the modern adjustable cockpit. The practical moves:
    <em>clearance</em> dimensions (doorways, seat width) are sized to the large
    percentile; <em>reach</em> dimensions (controls, shelves) to the small; and where the
    population's range is wide, <strong>adjustability</strong> replaces a fixed choice —
    the office chair, the car seat, the standing desk.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ergonomics</strong> — in Europe often called human factors — generalises this
    from size to fit between person and product: cognitive load, posture over time, force
    required, and the feedback loop between user and tool. The field grew out of both
    psychology and engineering (Lillian Gilbreth, psychologist and engineer, was an early
    contributor), and its regulatory power is visible in one famous failure: after the
    Three Mile Island nuclear accident of 1979, investigation focused partly on control-room
    design, and regulators required better instrumentation and training — a case study in
    how interface decisions become public safety. Design for safety has reusable moves:
    guard the hazard, fail visibly rather than silently, use interlocks and confirmations
    where error is costly, and lean on structured artefacts like the World Health
    Organization's surgical safety checklist — a designed object, as much as any chair.
    <strong>Inclusivity</strong> is the ethical and commercial extension of the same
    percentile thinking: roughly one person in six lives with a significant disability,
    and the classic finding is that designing for the edge case improves the product for
    everyone — curb cuts serve wheelchair users and parents with prams and travellers
    with suitcases; captions serve deaf audiences and everyone on a loud train.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "design for the average person" — measure the typical
  user and build for them. It sounds efficient, and it is quietly everywhere in bad
  products. But averages collapse variation: a product sized to the average height,
  average reach and average grip fits almost nobody on all three at once, because the
  same real person is rarely average twice. The correct move is designing the
  <em>range</em> — percentiles, clearance for the large, reach for the small,
  adjustability in between — which is also, not coincidentally, the move that makes a
  product inclusive.</p>

  <h2>Aesthetics, affordances and the psychology of obvious</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Aesthetics</strong> in design is not garnish — it does cognitive work. The
    <em>aesthetic–usability effect</em> (Kurosu and Kashimura, 1995) found people rate
    attractive interfaces as easier to use, and tolerate their flaws longer; beauty buys
    patience. But the deeper layer is <strong>affordance</strong>, Don Norman's
    load-bearing concept (adapted from psychologist J. J. Gibson): an affordance is the
    relationship between an object and a user that makes an action possible — a flat plate
    affords pushing, a handle affords pulling. Norman's refinement matters for practice:
    physical affordances must be paired with <strong>signifiers</strong> — visible markers
    that communicate the affordance (the painted "PUSH" bar) — because an affordance nobody
    can perceive is, functionally, absent. Hence the Norman door.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two more of Norman's concepts complete the psychology of obvious. <strong>Mapping</strong>:
    controls should correspond spatially to their effects — stove knobs laid out like the
    burners they fire. <strong>Constraints</strong>: make wrong actions impossible or
    unlikely — the USB-C plug that cannot be inserted wrong, the form that refuses invalid
    input politely. Hierarchy, meanwhile, is applied perception: <strong>contrast is the
    lever</strong> — size, weight, colour and whitespace all shout — and
    <strong>whitespace is structure</strong>, not emptiness: it groups related things and
    separates unrelated ones faster than borders ever will (the Gestalt law of proximity
    doing layout work). A design that shouts everything is whispering.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> aesthetics is decoration — a final coat applied after
  the real design is done. The evidence says otherwise: the aesthetic–usability effect
  shows beauty changing measured usability judgements, whitespace does grouping work that
  borders would do worse, and contrast decisions are hierarchy decisions about what a user
  sees first. The honest version of the truth is narrower: aesthetics cannot rescue a
  wrong structure — a beautiful Norman door is still a Norman door — but it is never "just"
  paint.</p>

  <h2>Sustainability: the six Rs</h2>
  <p>
    Design's decisions extend through the product's whole life, and the framework is the
    hierarchy of <strong>Rethink, Refuse, Reduce, Reuse, Repair, Recycle</strong> — ordered
    deliberately from most to least effective. <strong>Rethink</strong>: question the need —
    does the product need to exist in this form at all (a library of tools versus everyone
    owning a drill)? <strong>Refuse</strong>: design out the harmful part — no plastic film
    on a cucumber, no charger bundled with every device. <strong>Reduce</strong>: less
    material, lighter packaging, concentrated refills — the smallest change with the
    biggest volume effect. <strong>Reuse</strong>: build for a second life — refillable
    bottles, modular phones. <strong>Repair</strong>: the most design-active R, because
    repairability is decided at the drawing stage — screws instead of glue, standard parts,
    published manuals; the "right to repair" movement is design policy catching up with
    design decisions. <strong>Recycle</strong> comes last, honestly: it is the weakest R
    (most recycling is downcycling, and collection rates for plastics are dismal) — a
    useful reminder that the hierarchy exists because designers instinctively reach for
    the last R when the first one was the real answer. The counter-example that motivates
    the whole section is <strong>planned obsolescence</strong> — from the 1924 Phoebus
    light-bulb cartel (agreeing to shorten bulb life) to software that slows older
    hardware — design's shadow side, where the same skill that extends a product's useful
    life is used to curtail it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the chain so far runs from the job and its context, through
    the bodies that will use the product, through the psychology of what looks usable, to
    the product's life on the planet. The last two links widen out: what other designs the
    product stands on, and what its existence does to the society around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Existing designs, trends, culture — and design's impact</h2>
  <p>
    No designer starts from zero. <strong>Existing designs</strong> are the raw material:
    benchmarking the category (what do users already expect — the muscle memory of the
    back button), copying structure where convention serves users and breaking it only
    where breaking is justified. <strong>Fashion and trends</strong> move faster than
    ergonomics and must be handled accordingly — skeuomorphism gave way to flat design
    gave way to soft gradients, mostly as taste cycles, while the underlying human factors
    stayed constant; the discipline is knowing which layer a trend lives in.
    <strong>Society and different cultures</strong> enter everywhere: colour symbolism
    varies (white for weddings here, mourning there), reading direction flips layout logic
    (right-to-left scripts mirror navigation), icon conventions fail across borders (the
    mailbox, the check mark), and the highest-performing designs are culturally adapted,
    not just translated.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if it works for me, it works for everyone." It is the
  most natural assumption in design and the most expensive one, because the designer is
  definitionally not the typical user: expert in the product, on the newest hardware,
  inside one culture's conventions. The whole toolkit of this lesson is a defence against
  that assumption — personas built from research rather than introspection, percentiles
  rather than one body, cultural adaptation rather than translation, and (as the next
  section shows) testing with real users, whose behaviour routinely embarrasses the
  designer's certainties.</p>
  <p>
    And <strong>design's impact on society and markets</strong> is the reflexive question
    the syllabus insists on. Design creates markets (the smartphone was a designed
    convergence that made app economies possible), shapes behaviour (seatbelt chimes,
    default options — defaults are the most powerful "decision" a designer makes, as the
    behavioural-economics literature on organ-donation consent rates shows), and can
    weaponise the same psychology: <strong>dark patterns</strong> (a term coined by Harry
    Brignull, 2010) — confirm-shaming, hidden costs at checkout, roach motels that are
    easy to enter and designed to be hard to leave. The same Fitts's-law expertise that
    places the primary action under your thumb can place the "cancel subscription" link
    where your thumb never goes. Ethics is not adjacent to this subject; it is the same
    lever, aimed.
  </p>

  <h2>Never let a click land in silence: feedback engineering</h2>
  <p>
    The rules above converge on a feedback habit worth its own section, because it is
    where hierarchy and affordance meet time. Every user action needs a <em>visible
    result within roughly 100 milliseconds</em> — the perception threshold — or the system
    feels broken: buttons show a pressed state, forms disable their submit control while
    working, uploads show progress, and failures explain themselves. The subtlety is
    honest feedback: <em>optimistic UI</em> (showing the sent message instantly, rolling
    back on failure) trades truth for speed and must be engineered carefully — a message
    that quietly fails after appearing to send is worse than a visible spinner. Loading
    states are themselves decisions: a bare spinner over one second teaches users to
    distrust the system; a skeleton layout teaches them what is coming. And <strong>error
    messages are design</strong>, often the most-seen screen in a product: say what
    happened, why, and what to do next — "Card declined — try another card or contact
    your bank", not "Error 402".
  </p>
  <p>
    There is even quantitative evidence that decisions under load need designed support.
    One human-factors study of a bank's information-security centre examined about 45,000
    operator decisions over 60 days and found that as daily decision volume climbed,
    operators increasingly flagged harmless events as real breaches — cognitive effort
    changes error patterns in predictable ways. Interfaces are part of that system:
    surfaces that surface the signal, throttle forced decisions and confirm before
    destructive consequences are safety engineering, not decoration. One more hierarchy
    rule follows from all of it: <strong>one primary action per screen</strong> — if two
    buttons look equally important, the design has decided nothing. The test of feedback
    is brutal and cheap: use the product with sound off, from across the room, and ask
    whether the screen's state tells the story by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A cockpit sized to the average of five body measurements fit almost nobody. Why, and what two design strategies fix it?</li>
    <li>Why does a flat plate with the word PULL painted on it keep failing, in affordance-and-signifier terms?</li>
    <li>Rank Rethink, Repair and Recycle from most to least effective, and justify the order with one sentence each.</li>
    <li>Your app's submit request can take three seconds. What should the user see after 100 milliseconds, one second, and on failure?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Averages do not stack — being average on height, reach
    and grip together is rare, so the "average" cockpit fit nearly no one (Daniels's
    4,000-plus pilots). Fixes: design to percentiles (clearance for the large, reach for
    the small) and adjustability across the range. (2) The plate's shape affords pushing
    and the signifier (the printed PULL) contradicts it; an affordance users can perceive
    and a signifier must agree, or users trust the shape and the word loses — hence the
    Norman door. (3) Rethink first (questioning the need eliminates all downstream impact),
    Repair second (longevity is decided at the drawing stage — screws versus glue), Recycle
    last (it is mostly downcycling with poor collection rates, and it treats a symptom the
    earlier Rs could have prevented). (4) Within about 100 milliseconds a pressed/loading
    state (feedback before the perception threshold); at one second a skeleton or progress
    indication that says what is coming; on failure a message that says what happened, why
    and what to try next — never silence, because silent interfaces get retried, usually
    destructively.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Design is deciding on the user's behalf; the dominant decisions are hierarchy (what matters most), affordance (what looks usable) and feedback (confirming every action).</li>
    <li>Fitts's law: time to hit a target grows with the logarithm of distance over size — and its returns flatten, so know where to stop enlarging.</li>
    <li>User needs are a job under conditions: personas from research, jobs-to-be-done, and context of use (environment, frequency, consequences of error).</li>
    <li>Anthropometrics: design the 5th-to-95th percentile range, not the average; clearance for the large, reach for the small, adjustability in between; inclusive design improves products for everyone.</li>
    <li>Affordance needs a signifier; mapping and constraints prevent error; aesthetics does cognitive work but cannot rescue a wrong structure.</li>
    <li>Sustainability is a hierarchy — Rethink, Refuse, Reduce, Reuse, Repair, Recycle — from most to least effective; planned obsolescence is the shadow side of the same skill.</li>
    <li>Defaults are decisions; dark patterns are the lever aimed at the user; feedback must be visible within about 100 milliseconds, honest, and never silent — and one primary action per screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The chain of influences you have just walked is this unit's whole territory, and the
    next lesson goes deeper on one link: <em>accessibility</em> — designing deliberately
    for the full range of abilities, from standards and contrast ratios to screen readers,
    as the rigorous extension of the percentile thinking introduced here. Later units add
    design principles, research methods (the usability testing previewed below), and the
    communication and making skills that turn decisions into artefacts.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative layer, for the committed: <strong>Hick's law</strong> — reaction time
    grows with the logarithm of the number of choices, which is why menus shave options and
    why grouping choices into categories works (it lowers the effective choice count per
    decision); with Fitts's law it predicts interaction time almost mechanically, and
    together they justify the field's deepest interface habits: few, large, well-spaced
    targets. <strong>Feedback timing has thresholds</strong>: the classic numbers —
    perception of immediate response under about 100 milliseconds, a felt pause by about a
    second, abandonment risk past ten seconds — set the budget for loading states,
    optimistic UI and progress bars: never let a click land in silence, because silent
    interfaces get retried, usually destructively.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>And validation remains the field's honesty device</strong>: usability tests
    (the long-standing Nielsen–Landauer finding that around five users surface the large
    majority of severe usability problems — because usability problems are shared, not
    individually unique), analytics for what people <em>do</em>, interviews for why.
    <strong>Taste gets you to a candidate; evidence decides.</strong> The best designers
    are the ones most willing to be wrong early, on paper, where being wrong is free —
    sketches, prototypes, and the humility that the user's mental model, not the
    designer's, is the one the product has to live inside. Design is deciding; the
    discipline is deciding <em>well</em>, for bodies and contexts and cultures you did not
    come from, and being able to say why each decision was made — with a formula, an
    observation, or a percentile — when someone asks.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fitts (1954); Hick (1952); Norman, The Design of Everyday Things (1988); Nielsen & Landauer (1993); Kurosu & Kashimura (1995); Brignull on dark patterns (2010); UK Design & Technology syllabus treatment of the six Rs. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
